# Committed TEMPLATE documenting apps/server's dev env contract. The real `apps/server/.env` is
# GENERATED by `pnpm dev:setup` and gitignored — do not edit it by hand; regenerate with
# `pnpm dev:reset`. This file is only here to document each variable.
#
# ONBOARDING (slice 2a/2b): a box that boots with no `WAITRON_TILL_*_ID` set (below) enters SETUP
# MODE and mounts `/setup-api`. `POST /setup-api/provision` then GENERATES two more files under
# `WAITRON_STATE_DIR` (default `apps/server/src/state`, gitignored) — `secrets.env` (the vault key +
# node token, slice 2a) and `trading.env` (the five `WAITRON_TILL_*_ID` + `DATABASE_URL` +
# `WAITRON_MIGRATIONS_DATABASE_URL` + `WAITRON_ENV`, slice 2b) — and restarts the process. The
# supervisor must SOURCE both on the next boot for that restart to land in TRADING mode:
# `dev-server.mjs` (`pnpm dev`) does this by adding `--env-file=<stateDir>/secrets.env` and
# `--env-file=<stateDir>/trading.env` when those files exist; the appliance equivalent is systemd's
# `EnvironmentFile=-<stateDir>/secrets.env` + `EnvironmentFile=-<stateDir>/trading.env` (the leading
# `-` marks each optional). Do not hand-edit either generated file.
#
# OWNER CONNECTION REQUIRED IN SETUP MODE: while unprovisioned, `DATABASE_URL` (and
# `WAITRON_MIGRATIONS_DATABASE_URL` if set — it defaults to `DATABASE_URL`) is also the connection
# `POST /setup-api/provision` uses to run `applyVenue`, which INSERTs into `tenants` — a privilege
# `app_user` deliberately lacks (CLAUDE.md §3) but the table OWNER holds implicitly. So the setup-mode
# connection must be OWNER-capable, not the least-privilege app role: in dev that is the container
# `postgres` superuser below (`dev:onboard` writes exactly this URL), which owns everything and so can
# INSERT into `tenants`. On a real appliance the OWNER is the role that ran `waitron-provision
# instance`: that connection ran `CREATE DATABASE` and the migrations over the ADMIN string
# (`packages/provisioning/src/instance-apply.ts` — "ownership of the database is enough"), so it owns
# the tables. It is NOT `waitron_migrator` (`WAITRON_MIGRATIONS_DATABASE_URL`'s role): per
# `instance-plan.ts`'s REQUIREMENTS that role is a member of `app_user` with `CREATEROLE` +
# `GRANT CREATE ON DATABASE` grants but NO table ownership and NO INSERT on `tenants`. Wiring that
# owner connection on the appliance is deferred with the rest of the instance role-split (slice-2b R1);
# `boot.ts` uses `config.migrationsDatabaseUrl` today, which is correct ONLY because dev's superuser
# owns all.

# The local dev Postgres (docker-compose.yml), as the container superuser. Throwaway PREPRODUCTION
# data only — never a real database. In SETUP MODE this doubles as the owner connection provisioning
# runs `applyVenue` over — see the note above.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:pg@localhost:5432/postgres

# Deployment environment. Dev is ALWAYS preproduction — it never files a real AEAT record. Typing
# out `production` here against this throwaway database is exactly the mistake WAITRON_ENV guards.
WAITRON_ENV=preproduction

# The server's HTTP port. The till (5190) and dashboard (5191) Vite dev servers proxy `/api` here.
WAITRON_HTTP_PORT=8080

# WAITRON_STATE_DIR is where the box persists its self-signed cert, generated secrets.env, and (once
# provisioned) trading.env — see the ONBOARDING note above; unset uses a local default. Set
# WAITRON_TLS_CERT_FILE / WAITRON_TLS_KEY_FILE (both or neither) to override the self-signed cert
# with your own — the box still generates its secrets.
# WAITRON_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/waitron
# WAITRON_TLS_CERT_FILE=/etc/waitron/tls/cert.pem
# WAITRON_TLS_KEY_FILE=/etc/waitron/tls/key.pem

# Credentials-encryption key: 32 random bytes, base64, plus its version. `dev:setup` generates a
# fresh key each time it provisions.
WAITRON_CREDENTIALS_KEY=<base64-encoded-32-bytes>
WAITRON_CREDENTIALS_KEY_VERSION=1

# The provisioned till's fiscal identity — the five ids a venue mints (tenant, till, node/SIF,
# invoice series, location). `dev:setup` fills these from the venue it provisions.
WAITRON_TILL_TENANT_ID=<uuid>
WAITRON_TILL_TILL_ID=<uuid>
WAITRON_TILL_NODE_ID=<uuid>
WAITRON_TILL_SERIES_ID=<uuid>
WAITRON_TILL_LOCATION_ID=<uuid>
